// F17 -- `Class X projects Y { ... }` crashes the parser with a NullPointerException.
//
// Run:
//   java -cp <engine-runner>/target/classes:$(cat cp.txt) perf.ParseMain model.pure
//
// Observed:
//   REJECTED: An exception of type 'NullPointerException' occurred, please notify developer
//
// Expected: either the construct is supported, or it is declined the way `native` is --
// "Unsupported syntax", with source information pointing at the element.
//
// Cause: DomainParserGrammar has a full projection rule set --
//   classDefinition: ... (PROJECTS projection) | ...
//   projection: dsl | treePath
//   treePath: type alias? stereotypes? taggedValues? treePathClassBody
// -- so the text parses to a tree. DomainParseTreeWalker.visitClass then dereferences
// ctx.classBody() unconditionally, and the projection form has no classBody.
//
// Reachable by typing into any .pure file, which is what makes it worth reporting: a user
// gets a stack-trace-grade message with no line, no column and no construct named.
//
// The syntax itself is real and in use -- legend-engine ships
//   .../core_relational/relational/tests/testModel/projectionTestModel.pure
// which is written exactly this way and is parsed by legend-pure's own M3 grammar, not by
// Engine's DomainParserGrammar. So the two front ends disagree about a construct that
// exists in the repository's own model files.
###Pure
Class fixture::repro::Person
{
   firstName: String[1];
   lastName: String[1];
}

Class fixture::repro::PersonProjection projects
fixture::repro::Person
{
   +[firstName, lastName]
}
